$OctSixArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-shoe-box-size-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Shoe Box Size Labels: Make the Right Pair Easy to Pick'
    Description = 'Plan shoe box size and variant labels around shelf scanning, size systems, barcode placement, carton texture and reorder control.'
    Category = 'Footwear packaging'
    CardDescription = 'A great shoe box label should help the picker find the right size before the lid is opened.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of fully printed original shoe box size labels with clear size and color cues on stacked retail cartons'
    Caption = 'Show the size, color and approved SKU on the face a picker can actually see.'
    Published = '2026-10-06'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can one shoe box label cover every size system?', 'Only if the brand has approved a clear conversion and enough space. Do not invent US, UK or EU equivalents from a generic chart.'),
      @('Where should the barcode go?', 'Place it on a stable visible face with sufficient clear area, then scan the final printed label on a filled box in the real picking workflow.'),
      @('Will a removable label work on every shoe carton?', 'Coated, textured and recycled boards behave differently. Trial the specified construction on the production carton and confirm the desired removal behavior.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A wall of shoe boxes looks organized until a picker has to distinguish size 8 from 8.5 in a hurry. A beautiful side panel will not help if the size is buried under a small brand mark. I would start with the shelf view: which face stays visible when the boxes are stacked, and what does the worker need to read before touching one?</p>
  <p>The label is a compact data system, not just packaging decoration. Agree on the brand's size notation, color name, style code and approved SKU. If several markets use different size systems, the brand must supply the conversions. A printer should not improvise them from a web chart.</p>
  <h2>Make the Shelf Face Work First</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Design check</th><th>Picking consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Size</td><td>Read from standing distance</td><td>Wrong pair leaves the shelf</td></tr><tr><td>Color</td><td>Spell out similar shades</td><td>Variants get mixed</td></tr><tr><td>Style code</td><td>Use approved data</td><td>Reorder mismatch</td></tr><tr><td>Barcode</td><td>Scan on the finished box</td><td>Manual lookup slows packing</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>An original small shoe silhouette or color swatch can help someone orient quickly, but it should not outrank the size. Try the longest color name and the widest style code on the actual label width. Keep the barcode away from box folds, tape and heavy printed patterns.</p>
  <h2>Test the Carton, Not the Screen</h2>
  <p>Apply labels to the production shoe carton after the same handling the packing team uses. Coated paperboard, rough recycled stock and laminated finishes need different attention. Stack the boxes, rub the exposed edge and scan through the normal warehouse light. If the label must peel off for returns, check removal after realistic dwell time rather than immediately after application.</p>
  <p>Give the line one placement photo. A label that shifts between the short end and long side defeats the purpose of a standardized pick face. Record the approved print version with the data file so an old size layout cannot quietly return on a reorder.</p>
  <h2>Close the Loop Before Bulk Printing</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Photograph the stacked shelf and visible box face</li><li>Supply brand-approved size conversions and SKU data</li><li>Proof longest size and color combinations</li><li>Scan the barcode on a packed carton</li><li>Trial adhesion and any removal requirement</li><li>Save the label placement and version record</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/retail-price-stickers-markdown-guide/">retail price sticker guide</a> covers temporary shelf changes; the <a href="/blog/shipping-carton-identification-labels-guide/">carton ID guide</a> goes deeper on scan placement. Send a box photo and approved data fields through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. The best result is a picker who never needs to open the wrong box.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'subscription-box-variant-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Subscription Box Variant Stickers for Changing Monthly Packs'
    Description = 'Plan subscription box variant stickers around changing assortments, pick accuracy, month codes, box surfaces and packing checks.'
    Category = 'Subscription fulfillment'
    CardDescription = 'The assortment changes faster than the outer carton; the sticker should keep packing clear.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of subscription cartons with distinct original illustrated monthly variant stickers on a packing bench'
    Caption = 'A visible variant cue helps the packing team separate boxes without opening every one.'
    Published = '2026-10-06'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should the sticker list every item inside a subscription box?', 'Only if the business owns a stable approved assortment and needs that information outside. A short pack code may be safer when contents can change.'),
      @('Can colors alone identify variants?', 'Color helps at a glance but should not be the only identifier. Include legible text or a code that matches the packing list.'),
      @('How can old monthly stickers be prevented from being used?', 'Use an approved version or cycle code, segregate obsolete rolls, and reconcile labels with the packing plan before each run.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>The plain outer box survives from one month to the next. The contents do not. That is where a subscription variant sticker earns its place: it tells the packer which version is in hand without forcing them to reopen a sealed carton. I would design the label with the packing list beside it, not with the marketing image alone.</p>
  <p>First decide which information actually changes. It may be a month, a region, a gift option or a customer-selected theme. The fulfillment owner supplies the approved variant codes and decides whether external text can reveal the contents. Do not promise a fixed assortment on the label if substitutions remain possible.</p>
  <h2>Keep Variants Distinct Under Packing Pressure</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Practical check</th><th>Risk if missed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Variant name</td><td>Read from the packing position</td><td>Wrong kit is sealed</td></tr><tr><td>Cycle code</td><td>Match the current pick sheet</td><td>Old month is shipped</td></tr><tr><td>Color cue</td><td>Pair with text</td><td>Lookalike variants mix</td></tr><tr><td>Placement</td><td>See on a stacked box</td><td>Workers turn every carton</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>A set of original illustrated icons can distinguish themes, but the text and approved code must remain clear. Test the labels from the distance and angle of a real packing bench. If the box is kraft or heavily printed, check contrast on the actual stock.</p>
  <h2>Control the Changeover</h2>
  <p>Print a small proof batch with the longest variant name. Apply it after normal box assembly, rub the edge and see whether the sticker lands across a fold or tape line. At changeover, remove obsolete rolls from the station. One leftover strip of last month's artwork can create a customer-service problem much larger than the label cost.</p>
  <p>The label is only one check. The packing team still needs a versioned pick list, a clear station setup and a way to flag mismatches. Agree who signs off the first packed sample and how the last box is reconciled. Do not turn a decorative sticker into an unverified inventory system.</p>
  <h2>Ask for a Proof That Matches the Operation</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Provide the current assortment or variant codes</li><li>Mark the visible face of the assembled box</li><li>Use text as well as original color or icons</li><li>Proof the longest variant and month code</li><li>Check old-roll segregation at changeover</li><li>Approve the first packed carton against the list</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> for version control and the <a href="/blog/custom-shipping-mailer-seal-stickers-guide/">mailer seal guide</a> when the sticker also closes a flap. Send your carton and packing workflow through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. The design should make the right box the easy choice.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'repair-status-stickers-equipment-guide'
    Title = 'Repair Status Stickers for Equipment Without Residue Surprises'
    Description = 'Plan removable repair status stickers for devices and tools around clean placement, writable fields, hand-offs, surface testing and privacy.'
    Category = 'Service operations'
    CardDescription = 'A service label needs to survive the bench, then leave the equipment in acceptable condition.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original printed repair status labels with writable service fields on unbranded equipment at a workbench'
    Caption = 'Illustrative job numbers are shown. Keep status fields clear without covering vents, controls or existing identifiers.'
    Published = '2026-10-06'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a removable sticker be placed on any device finish?', 'No. Coatings, textured plastics and aged paint vary. Trial the label on representative equipment and confirm acceptable removal.'),
      @('Should a repair label include customer contact details?', 'Use only information the service operator needs and is authorized to display. A job reference may be preferable to personal details on exposed equipment.'),
      @('Can the sticker cover a serial plate?', 'Avoid covering existing serial numbers, safety markings, vents or controls. Choose a separate stable panel and approve placement with the equipment owner.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>On a busy service bench, two similar devices can sit side by side with different problems and different owners. A repair status sticker is valuable because it makes the next hand-off explicit. I would put the job reference and present status first. A cute tool icon is fine, but it cannot be the only thing a technician sees.</p>
  <p>Agree which fields are printed and which are written or overprinted: job ID, received date, stage, technician initials and any short handling note. Avoid exposed personal details unless the service operator has a clear need and permission. The label maker should not invent a privacy policy or a repair decision.</p>
  <h2>Pick a Surface That Will Not Cause Another Repair</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Potential issue</th><th>Better check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Painted housing</td><td>Finish may lift on removal</td><td>Trial after real dwell time</td></tr><tr><td>Vent or control</td><td>Function may be blocked</td><td>Choose a separate panel</td></tr><tr><td>Serial plate</td><td>Identity becomes hidden</td><td>Keep existing ID visible</td></tr><tr><td>Carry handle</td><td>Frequent rubbing</td><td>Inspect during normal handling</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Test the intended pen or printer on the final face stock. A handwritten status that smears under workshop handling is worse than no status. Use original small status symbols only alongside readable words, especially if multiple teams work on the same job.</p>
  <h2>Follow the Sticker Through the Job</h2>
  <p>Apply samples to representative equipment using the normal intake routine. Handle, store and clean the item as usual, then remove the label after the expected service interval. Check residue, finish change and whether a label fragment remains near a control. Different device surfaces may need different attachment methods. Never imply one adhesive is universally safe.</p>
  <p>Give the team a rule for status changes. Crossing out an old stage and writing a new one can be acceptable if the field is designed for it; piling labels on top of each other makes the last state ambiguous. The service operator owns the workflow and the final equipment check.</p>
  <h2>Make the Hand-Off Readable</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>List the minimum visible job fields</li><li>Mark approved placement away from controls</li><li>Test writing or variable printing at final size</li><li>Trial wear and removal after real dwell time</li><li>Decide how status changes are recorded</li><li>Keep customer details off exposed labels unless needed</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable adhesive guide</a> frames the removal test, while the <a href="/blog/custom-electronics-serial-number-labels-guide/">serial label guide</a> explains identifier discipline. Send equipment photos and field requirements through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The best service label keeps a job moving without leaving a mark behind.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-cable-wrap-id-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Cable Wrap ID Labels: Readable After Installation'
    Description = 'Plan wraparound cable identification labels for installed equipment with diameter, print orientation, small text, handling and owner-approved IDs.'
    Category = 'Equipment identification'
    CardDescription = 'An ID that looks clear on a flat proof can disappear when wrapped behind the cable.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of complete original printed cable ID wrap labels on unbranded equipment cables beside a labeled roll'
    Caption = 'Proof the identifier on the actual cable diameter and from the side a technician will see.'
    Published = '2026-10-06'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can one wrap label fit every cable diameter?', 'No. The printable area, wrap length and final overlap change with cable diameter. Test the exact sizes in the installation set.'),
      @('Who defines the cable IDs?', 'The equipment or installation owner supplies the approved identifiers and orientation convention. The printer must not invent technical circuits or safety meanings.'),
      @('Is a flat digital proof enough?', 'No. Wrap a physical printed sample around the cable, then check readability, edge hold and scan behavior if codes are used.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>Before installation, a cable ID can look generous on a flat screen. After installation, it may sit behind a bundle with only a narrow strip visible. I would proof the label on the actual cable diameter and look at it from the technician's working position. That simple move catches more mistakes than another round of zooming into the artwork file.</p>
  <p>The owner supplies the cable IDs, circuit meanings and any site-specific identification rules. The label supplier can plan print orientation, material and application, but should not assign technical meaning to a conductor or claim compliance without a verified specification.</p>
  <h2>Measure the Wrapped Result</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Physical check</th><th>Failure to avoid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cable diameter</td><td>Wrap smallest and largest cable</td><td>ID disappears in overlap</td></tr><tr><td>Print direction</td><td>Read from service side</td><td>Text faces the wall</td></tr><tr><td>Identifier length</td><td>Use longest approved ID</td><td>Type becomes tiny</td></tr><tr><td>Edge position</td><td>Handle the bundle normally</td><td>Corner lifts or snags</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Use a clear original geometric mark or color band only as a secondary cue. Keep the approved identifier in high contrast. If the label has a clear overlaminating tail, confirm the printed area remains visible after that tail wraps over it. A barcode or QR code must be scan-tested in its final curved position, not assumed from a screen image.</p>
  <h2>Trial the Installation Conditions</h2>
  <p>Ask whether the cable is handled indoors, near heat sources, in an oily workshop or in an area with routine cleaning. Trial the proposed construction under the actual use conditions. Some applications may require a different identification method altogether. A generic durability promise is not an engineering specification.</p>
  <p>Record the application point relative to connectors and bends. Installers need enough free cable to wrap consistently without covering manufacturer markings or making maintenance harder. Save a wrapped reference sample with the approved data file for repeat production.</p>
  <h2>Approve the Cable, Not Just the Roll</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Provide cable diameters and photos in position</li><li>Supply owner-approved identifiers</li><li>Proof longest ID at final wrap size</li><li>Check viewing and print orientation</li><li>Test edge hold after normal handling</li><li>Scan curved codes if they are required</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/white-ink-small-text-custom-stickers/">small-text guide</a> helps with legibility and the <a href="/blog/qr-code-stickers-scan-testing-guide/">scan guide</a> covers code verification. Send cable sizes and an annotated application photo through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A usable ID is one a technician can find without untangling the whole bundle.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'flat-pack-furniture-parts-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Flat-Pack Furniture Parts Stickers That Survive Assembly'
    Description = 'Plan furniture part identification stickers around panel finishes, A/B part codes, packing sequence, assembly visibility and clean removal.'
    Category = 'Furniture assembly'
    CardDescription = 'Part A is only useful when the assembler can still find it after the box is opened.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated A and B part identification stickers on flat-pack furniture panels with an assembly sheet'
    Caption = 'Place the part ID where it survives packing but can be removed from the finished surface.'
    Published = '2026-10-06'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a parts sticker go on every furniture finish?', 'No. Veneer, paint, laminate and unfinished wood respond differently. Trial the adhesive and removal on each representative finish.'),
      @('Should the label stay on during assembly?', 'That depends on the workflow. It should remain visible long enough to identify the part but not be trapped under hardware or visible on the finished product.'),
      @('Who supplies A and B part codes?', 'The furniture maker owns the bill of materials and assembly instructions. Label artwork must match those approved part references.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A flat-pack box opens onto several panels that look almost identical. The instruction sheet says "Part B," and the person building the shelf turns each board over to find it. I would treat the part sticker as part of the assembly experience, not as a warehouse afterthought. It has to be present when needed and gone when the furniture is finished.</p>
  <p>The furniture maker must supply the approved part codes and match them to the bill of materials. A printer can make A, B and C clear, but it cannot resolve a mismatch in the assembly instructions. Confirm whether the sticker belongs on a panel edge, a protective wrap or a removable backing sheet.</p>
  <h2>Choose the Placement With the Box Open</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Question</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Finished face</td><td>Can it be removed cleanly?</td><td>Visible residue</td></tr><tr><td>Raw board edge</td><td>Will it hold in transit?</td><td>Lost ID</td></tr><tr><td>Protective film</td><td>Is film removed early?</td><td>Part loses identity</td></tr><tr><td>Hardware bag</td><td>Does the code match the sheet?</td><td>Wrong fittings used</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>A complete original little panel silhouette can help distinguish similar parts, but the letter and approved code should be largest. Try the artwork at the scale of the actual part. An assembler should not need a magnifier to separate B from D.</p>
  <h2>Run an Unboxing and Removal Trial</h2>
  <p>Pack representative panels in the planned order, then open the carton as a customer would. Can each part still be identified without rearranging the entire pile? Rub the labels through normal handling, assemble the trial item and remove the stickers at the intended stage. Check every finish involved: veneer, laminate, paint and raw edge can respond differently.</p>
  <p>Do not promise residue-free removal based on one fresh sample. Dwell time, temperature and coating age matter. If direct application is risky, consider putting the ID on protective wrapping instead. The furniture owner decides acceptable placement and removal criteria.</p>
  <h2>Keep the Parts List in Sync</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Supply the approved bill of materials and part codes</li><li>Photograph the unboxing sequence</li><li>Mark safe label or wrap positions</li><li>Proof lookalike letters at final size</li><li>Trial hold during packing and removal after assembly</li><li>Version the labels with the instruction sheet</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable adhesive guide</a> before choosing a face stock, and use the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> to keep changed parts in step with changed instructions. Send panel photos and your part list through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The label should disappear only after it has done its job.</p>
'@
  }
)
